==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|Mephisto}}
* {{alter|en|Mephostopheles}} ''(dated)''

===Etymology===
The name ''Mephistopheles'' is a corrupted Greek compound.<ref name=":0">{{Cite book|last=Snider|first=Denton Jaques|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=um8oAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA132|title=Goethe's Faust: A Commentary|date=1886|publisher=Sigma|pages=132-133|language=en}}</ref> The Greek particle of negation (μη, ''mē'') and the Greek word for love or loving (φίλος, ''philos'') are the first and last terms of the compound but the middle term is more doubtful. For the middle term, three meanings have been noticed and three different complete etymologies have been established: 1) not loving light (φως, ''phos''; the old form of the word being ''Mephostopheles''), 2) not loving Faust, 3) allied to ''[[wikt:mephitic|mephitic]],'' a term which designates the poisonous vapors arising from the earth in certain places pools caverns springs destructive of human life.<ref name=":0" />

